L177: 1Diets were prepared by Research Diets (New Brunswick, NJ). The study mice (WT and Ttpa-/-) were fed an αT deficient diet for 12 weeks after weaning. Ttpa breeders were fed the LOW diet to require breeder reproduction but also minimize αT transfer to offspring.
L178: 2αT in soybean oil was 2.92 mg/kg diet via HPLC (Study mice should have received daily 5.8-8.8 ng αT derived from soybean oil based on average food intake, 2-3 grams/day).
L179: 3AIN-93 mineral mix for growing rodents (S10022G).
L180: 4AIN-93 vitamin mix with no vitamin E (V13402).
L181: 5The purity of αTA in the oil was analyzed via HPLC before diet production. The original purity from the company was 92.7 %, while the purity via HPLC was 80.3 %. We combined both purities to calculate the average αTA concentration.
L182: 6αTA levels were below the limit of detection (0.49 mg/kg) in the αT deficient diet.
L183: Abbreviations: αT, α-tocopherol; αTA, α-tocopherol acetate; LOW, low vitamin E diet.
L184: Supplemental Figure 1. Breeding and study design protocol.
L185: Male Ttpa-/- and control WT littermates were generated using a trio-breeder strategy. The breeders were fed LOW diet. At weaning, the study mice were fed VED diet for 12 weeks. Abbreviations: LOW, low α-tocopherol; Ttpa, α-tocopherol transfer protein; VED, α-tocopherol deficient; WT, wild-type
